README-VI -- two vi editors, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media, you want ONEvi.  This file is here so you do not have to install both and try them.Short answer: take `elvis' unless you have a reason not to.  It has its wholemanual on this disk, its whole source, and it brings a read-only `view' and afew helpers with it.  Take `vi' instead if you want the small plain one thatasks for nothing but a termcap, or if you cannot give an editor a /dd/tmp.THE FILES ARE TWO EDITORS-------------------------  file                  bytes  needs   module   source      docs                                cio     name  ---------------------------------------------------------------------  vi                    81468   no      vi      SRC/pvic    -  REBUILT/vi_1.0        78496   no      vi_1.0  SRC/pvic    -  elvis                111944   no      elvis   65 files    14 files  view                  15132   no      view    with elvis  with elvis  REBUILT/vi.elvis      15140   no      vi.elvis  with elvis  with elvis  PVIC       vi, REBUILT/vi_1.0             two builds of one program  Elvis 1.7  elvis, view, REBUILT/vi.elvis  one program, three namesNeither needs a Microware module: both start on a disk with no `cio' present.WHAT EACH ONE NEEDS BESIDES ITSELF----------------------------------This matters if you are copying one onto your own media, because elvis willnot start on a /dd that is missing a directory.  Read from the binaries:  vi        /dd/sys/termcap  vi_1.0    /dd/sys/termcap  elvis     /dd/tmp   /h0/sys/termcap   also /dd/SYS/password  view      -- it execs elvis, so it needs whatever elvis needs  vi.elvis  -- likewise`/dd/tmp' IS NOT OPTIONAL for elvis.  The path is compiled in, and withoutthe directory it stops before it draws anything:    Can't create temp file... Does directory "/dd/tmp" exist?Measured 2026-08-31, by taking /dd/tmp away and running it.  This disk shipsa /dd/tmp, so it does not bite here -- it bites on the machine you copy theeditor TO.  Two ways out, and the second was measured the same day:  - make the directory: `makdir /dd/tmp', publicly writable  - point the editor somewhere else, before it opens anything:        setenv EXINIT "set directory=/dd/USR"    elvis reads EXINIT and .exrc before it creates the temp file, so this    works; the same line in an .exrc does too.The termcap entries are read from the TERMCAP environment variable first, soa /h0 is not required -- SYS/login sets it.  On a terminal neither editorknows, source the entry first: `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry'.ONE MODULE NAME ANSWERS FOR ONE PROGRAM---------------------------------------OS-9 finds a program by MODULE name once it is resident, whatever path youtype, so two files carrying one module name are two paths to one program.Every file above now states its own name, and that is deliberate: it was notalways so.Measured 2026-08-30, before those renames: `/dd/CMDS/REBUILT/VI' opened a fileand filled the screen; `load /dd/CMDS/vi', run that same path again, and yougot a different editor.  Nothing warned you.  If you add a vi of your ownbeside these, give its module its own name.vi -- the small plain one-------------------------  PVIC 1.0a, the Portable VI Clone, public domain -- the author says so in  the program's own `About PVIC' screen.  Source in SRC/pvic.  The core vi command set and the common options (autoindent, ignorecase,  showmatch, tabstop) without wrapmargin, shiftwidth, paragraphs or sections.  It has ex mode, and it needs no Microware module.  `REBUILT/vi_1.0' is PVIC 1.0, an older edition of the same program kept  under its version number because `vi' was taken when it arrived.  Costs: no manual on this disk beyond its own help screen.  To leave it: ESC to get out of insert mode, then `:q!' and Enter to quit  without saving, or `:wq' to save and quit.elvis -- the one with everything around it------------------------------------------  Elvis 1.7 by Steve Kirkendall, ported by Peter Reinig, built here from  CMDS/archives/elvis1.7.lzh.  A clone: very close, not identical.  58 `:set' options, the most of the two, and the only one here with colour  and attribute handling (`charattr'), digraphs and a configurable `equalprg'.  It arrives as a family:      view              elvis opened read-only      ref               look a C function up from a tags file      fmt               format text      elvprsv, elvrec   preserve and recover a session across a crash      REBUILT/vi.elvis, elvis_input, ctags.elvis  `view' and `vi.elvis' are small wrappers that exec `elvis', so they need  CMDS/elvis present.  If you copy elvis, copy those you want with it.  Costs: the larger of the two, a clone rather than an original, and it wants  a /dd/tmp.  Best documented by a wide margin -- DOC/elvis holds the full man page set,  and SRC/elvis the whole 65-file source.  To leave it: the same keys -- ESC, then `:q!' or `:wq' and Enter.CHOOSING--------  You want a manual to read when something misbehaves    elvis  You want the plainest thing that edits                 vi  You cannot give an editor a /dd/tmp                    vi  You want read-only browsing                            view (needs elvis)  Either edits this disk's CR-terminated text correctly; both are OS-9 native  and neither will mangle a line ending.  If you take exactly one file and nothing else: `elvis' -- and give it a  /dd/tmp, or an EXINIT naming a directory you do have.  If you cannot make  a directory for it, `vi' is the one that asks for nothing but a termcap.